<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Ferrucci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1489–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Francesco Ferruccio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Ferrucci (1489–1530) was a Florentine military commander during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1529–1530, when the last republic resisted the imperial and papal armies restoring the Medici. Leading a relief army from Volterra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, he was defeated and captured at Gavinana on 3 August 1530 and killed by the imperial captain Fabrizio Maramaldo. He later became a hero of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-ferrucci.jpg" alt="Francesco Ferrucci" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uffizi_16,_Francesco_Ferrucci.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
